.TH KOSH 5 "2026-10-25" "kosh" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
kosh.conf \- Koshka settings files and the KOSHCONF encoding
.SH DESCRIPTION
Koshka settings files hold option values. Every shell loads
them before any startup file, interactive or not, and the configured mood also
applies to
.B kosh \-c
and to a script without a shebang. The
.B koshconf
builtin of
.BR kosh (1)
loads, changes, and writes them, and the OPTION REGISTRY section there lists
the options.
.PP
STARTUP FILES in
.BR kosh (1)
lists the shells that apply no settings file or KOSHCONF.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR /etc/kosh.conf " or " %ProgramData%\ekosh\ekosh.conf
The system settings file, loaded first and only when no other user could have
written it. On POSIX, the file and its directory must be owned by root and not
writable by group or others. On Windows, the file and its
.I kosh
directory must be owned by SYSTEM, Administrators, or TrustedInstaller, and no
access control entry may let another account modify them, change their
permissions, or take ownership. The directory may let others add files. A file
that fails the check is skipped with a warning.
.TP
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/kosh.conf " or " ~/.config/kosh/kosh.conf
The user settings file, loaded second. XDG_CONFIG_HOME applies when it is an
absolute path, and on Windows the directory falls back to USERPROFILE when HOME
is unset. On POSIX, a shell whose effective user is root neither loads nor
writes it.
.BR "koshconf create" ,
.BR "koshconf set \-\-persist" ,
and
.B koshconf save \-\-persist
write it. A symbolic link is preserved and its target is written. Existing
permission bits are kept, and a new file receives mode 0644 less the creation
mask. Missing directories are created. The previous file is copied to
.I kosh.conf.bak
beside it.
.TP
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/scripts/rc.kosh " or " ~/.config/kosh/scripts/rc.kosh
The user rc script of a login or interactive
.B kosh
mood, described under RC SCRIPT.
.PP
The Bash and POSIX startup files that the other moods source are listed in
.BR kosh (1).
.SH SYNTAX
A settings file is a sequence of lines ended by LF or CRLF, and a leading UTF-8
byte order mark is skipped. A line that is blank or starts with a number sign
after optional blanks is ignored. Every other line has the form
.IR name = value ,
with blanks around the name and value ignored. A value starting with a double
or single quote ends at the next quote of that kind, and only blanks may follow
it, so a value whose surrounding blanks must be kept needs quotes. A value
cannot span lines.
.PP
A boolean is on or off, also accepted as true, false, 1, or 0. An enumeration
is written as its value. A string must be valid UTF-8 without a NUL byte.
editor.history.max_entries is an integer from 0 to 2147483647, and
editor.wrap_width one from 0 to 65535. init_moods is a comma-separated list of
moods without empty elements. A name may be the canonical name or its legacy
alias.
.PP
A file may set every option except those fixed by how the shell started, such
as bash.interpreter.login_shell, and those that follow the session, such as
posix.interpreter.job_control, as OPTION REGISTRY in
.BR kosh (1)
lists them. A semantic option in a file changes every shell that loads it,
scripts included. Unless every script should use such an option, it can be set
for a session with
.BR set ,
.BR shopt ,
or
.BR "koshconf set" .
.PP
The mood applies first, wherever it appears, and the other lines are read
under it. In the
.B kosh
mood, a line that sets another value for an option the mood holds, as MOODS in
.BR kosh (1)
lists them, is skipped with a warning. A later line replaces an earlier one,
and the user file and KOSHCONF replace the system file. Options given on the
command line and an inherited KOSH_HISTORY_FILE or KOSH_HISTORY_SIZE take
precedence over a file, as OPTIONS and HISTORY in
.BR kosh (1)
describe.
.PP
.B koshconf
quotes a written value that starts with a quote or starts or ends with a blank,
and keeps the CRLF endings of a file that uses them. A value holding a newline,
or one that needs quoting and holds both quote characters, cannot be written,
and
.B koshconf set \-\-persist
rejects it before changing the session. Persisting mood=kosh also removes the
lines of the options the
.B kosh
mood fixes. A line whose option this version does not recognize is kept at the
end of a rewritten file.
.PP
.B koshconf create
writes every option permitted in a settings file except init_moods. Each
option has a help comment and its preset value. Only the mood is active,
and the remaining options are commented out, allowing the system settings
to apply until they are uncommented. A string option without a default has
an empty commented-out value. The
.B kosh
preset uses the values fixed by that mood, so uncommenting those options
produces no warning.
.SH KOSHCONF
.B KOSHCONF
is a dynamic shell variable holding the mood and the interactive options in a
binary form encoded as standard base64 with padding. It never carries
editor.history.file_path, posix.interpreter.job_control,
bash.editor.history.bang_expansion, bash.editor.history.records_commands, or
bash.interpreter.login_shell. An assignment to it is ignored. It is not
exported unless the user exports it, and after
.BR "export KOSHCONF" ,
each started command receives the settings current at its start. A prefix
assignment such as KOSHCONF=x before a command also passes the current
settings to that command. A starting
shell applies a KOSHCONF from its environment after the settings files and
removes it before any command runs, and
.B koshconf load
applies a value to a running shell. The variable carries the settings to a
remote shell in this way.
.PP
.RS
.EX
ssh host KOSHCONF="$KOSHCONF" kosh
.EE
.RE
.SS Encoding format
The decoded bytes are a sequence of records, each an option id, a value
length, and the value. The id and the length are unsigned LEB128 varints, with
seven bits per byte, the least significant group first, and the high bit set on
every byte but the last. A boolean is one byte, 0 or 1, an enumeration is a
varint holding the position of the value in its list, an integer is its
decimal digits in ASCII, and a string is its UTF-8 bytes. The ids are listed
under OPTION REGISTRY in
.BR kosh (1)
and never change meaning. Semantic options never pass through the environment,
and a child shell loads the settings files itself.
.PP
A record with an unknown id, an option the encoding never carries, or a value a
settings file would reject is skipped with a warning. Only the canonical form
is accepted, so the base64 text carries its padding, every varint uses the
fewest bytes, and the ids strictly ascend. A value that breaks these rules, or
has a truncated record, is rejected as a whole with a warning.
.SH ERRORS
A missing settings file is skipped. An unreadable file, a file over 1 MiB, one
that is not a regular file, and a system file that fails the ownership check
produce a warning and are skipped. A line without an equals sign, an unknown
option, an invalid value, an unclosed quote, text after a closing quote, and an
option a file cannot hold each produce a warning with the file and line, and
the line is skipped. A settings file never stops the shell.
.SH RC SCRIPT
A login or interactive shell in the
.B kosh
mood sources scripts/rc.kosh in the user configuration directory after the
settings files. The script can test
.B shopt \-q login_shell
for login commands. No system rc script, ~/.koshrc, or /etc/koshrc is read.
STARTUP FILES in
.BR kosh (1)
describes when the script runs and the options that replace or skip it. Shell
code belongs in the script, and options in
.IR kosh.conf .
.PP
.B koshconf create
writes the following script when it is missing and never replaces it.
.PP
.RS
.EX
#!/usr/bin/env kosh
if shopt \-q login_shell; then
  [ \-r /etc/profile ] && . /etc/profile
  if [ \-r ~/.bash_profile ]; then
    . ~/.bash_profile
  elif [ \-r ~/.bash_login ]; then
    . ~/.bash_login
  elif [ \-r ~/.profile ]; then
    . ~/.profile
  fi
else
  [ \-r ~/.profile ] && . ~/.profile
  [ \-r ~/.bashrc ] && . ~/.bashrc
fi
.EE
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
A user file that keeps the Bash grammar and edits with vi keys is
.PP
.RS
.EX
mood=bash
editor.history.max_entries=20000
editor.base_mode=vi
.EE
.RE
.PP
A user file that keeps the
.B kosh
mood and sources ~/.bashrc in the
.B bash
mood at interactive startup is
.PP
.RS
.EX
init_moods=bash
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B koshconf set
.I option value
.B \-\-persist
writes the same lines.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kosh (1)
